## Migration Analysis

Your code has **four** Thread.Abort-related APIs that will throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` or `SYSLIB0006` on .NET 8:

| Location | Pattern | Replacement |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` in `Stop()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in `Stop()` | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne()` or `Join(timeout)` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can wake up
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to the loop
                // condition check, which will exit cleanly.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals the token — cooperative, not forced

        // Give the thread up to 2 seconds to finish gracefully
        if (_workerThread is not null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            _workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

## Key Changes to Your Helper Methods

Your `GetNextItem()` likely blocks (comment says "may block"). You **must** thread the token through or it will never wake up on cancellation:

```csharp
// BEFORE — blocks indefinitely, Thread.Abort was the only escape
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// AFTER — wakes up immediately when token is cancelled
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken) throws
// OperationCanceledException when cancelled
```

If you use `Thread.Sleep` anywhere in `ProcessItem`:

```csharp
// BEFORE
Thread.Sleep(5000);

// AFTER — cancellation-aware wait
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(5000);
```

## Critical Pitfalls

1. **Token must reach blocking calls.** If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a socket/queue without the token, `Cancel()` won't interrupt it — the thread hangs until the next natural unblock. Pass the token all the way down.

2. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException` broadly.** The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not an unrelated one from inner code.

3. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It allocates a `WaitHandle` and timer internally. The `IDisposable` implementation above handles this.

4. **Cancellation is not immediate.** Unlike `Thread.Abort` (which injected an exception anywhere), cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` does 30 seconds of CPU work with no token check, cancellation waits. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive steps if needed.

5. **`Thread.ResetAbort` has no equivalent** — and none is needed. With cooperative cancellation you control the flow: the `catch` handles cleanup, `finally` runs `FlushBuffers()`, and the `while` condition exits the loop naturally.
